ation URL requested `ACTIVITY:READ` and `ACTIVITY:WRITE` together, and likewise both calendar scopes. Intervals treats WRITE as including READ and rejects repeated areas with “Duplicate scope.” The scope is now `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`.\n3. The server assumed a one-hour expiry when the token response omitted `expires_in`, then its refresher marked the connection expired because there was no `refresh_token`. Intervals access tokens have neither field and remain valid until revoked. The expiry and refresh machinery are removed. A 401 from the Intervals API now clears the encrypted token and exposes a reconnect state.\n4. The token exchange also sent `grant_type` and `redirect_uri` even though Intervals expects only `client_id`, `client_secret`, and `code`. It now sends exactly those fields and uses the returned `athlete.id`. The callback validates a signed, 15-minute JWT state with the app user id and purpose, and rejects a different athlete id on reconnect.\n\nThe real account flow and revocation check require configured OAuth credentials and an Intervals account; they cannot be completed from this local fixture.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"agent_message","text":"Fixed the Intervals.icu integration. The Connect button now uses an authenticated POST; scopes no longer repeat an area; token exchange sends only the required fields; and connections no longer expire after an invented one-hour TTL. A provider 401 now clears the encrypted token and prompts reconnection. The callback also validates signed state and checks for an athlete account mismatch.\n\nThe causes are documented in [ANSWER.md](/tmp/h-o3CM7O/ANSWER.md). All five local tests pass. A live OAuth and revocation check still requires real Intervals credentials and an athlete account."}}
